---
import ProjectPost from "../../../components/ProjectPost.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import cover from "./20260314-1.png";

export const meta = {
    title: "A festival site and a live VJ feed for Sultry Mist 2026",
    date: new Date(2026, 2, 14),
    description: "A phone backstage, Hydra, and a screen for the crowd",
    tech: ["hydrajs", "astro"],
    role: ["Engineering"],
    image: cover,
};
---

<ProjectPost meta={meta}>
    <p>
        I built <a href="https://sultrymist.com">sultrymist.com</a> for the 2026 edition
        of Sultry Mist festival in Aldona, Goa. It was a basic Astro website with
        lots of images. They wanted the colors to change and the background to have
        a moving dot effect, something &quot;dance-y&quot;.
    </p><p>
        I built the background with a dot matrix pattern and an infinite looped
        animation using pure CSS and the color shift on interaction was made
        using <code>@property</code> and some JavaScript.
    </p><p><Image src={cover} alt="" /></p><h2>The installation</h2><p>
        VDO.ninja handled the peer-to-peer video transport. A phone backstage
        acted as the camera source. On the display end, the stream was pulled
        into a browser via VDO.ninja's viewer URL. Since <code
            >s0.initStream()</code
        > is broken in Hydra for some reason I could not debug, I used <code
            >s0.initScreen()</code
        > and <code>OBS Studio</code> to create the display.
    </p><p>
        The output ran fullscreen on a display pointed at the audience, bridging
        the backstage energy with the crowd without any dedicated streaming
        infrastructure or paid tooling.
    </p><p>
        Here's a <a
            href="https://hydra.ojack.xyz/?"
            >preview of how one of the scenes looked.</a>
    </p>
</ProjectPost>
